35,2,1,talk,Shizue,,"The closeout files are sobering. Catalogue-style work lasts, but mechanism-focused projects often stall with questions unresolved.","{""words"":16,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
36,2,1,talk,Ryuji,,"The staffing data is alarming. Most phenotyping technicians are gone, and our senior modelling analysts are seconded abroad.","{""words"":18,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
37,2,1,talk,Victoria,,The spectroscopy suite was idle for eleven weeks. One engineer covers three institutes. This is a critical bottleneck.,"{""words"":18,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
38,2,1,talk,Benjamin,,"Simulation hours are free now, but animal-unit day rates have spiked. This changes the calculus for wet-lab heavy proposals.","{""words"":19,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
39,2,1,talk,Midori,,The field sites are a disaster. Three of five lost a season to weather. But the indoor holding rooms were fine.,"{""words"":21,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
40,2,2,talk,Shizue,,"@Ryuji, that explains why the mechanism-focused projects failed. We lack the technical hands to sustain them.","{""words"":16,""mentions"":[""Ryuji""],""raw_differs"":false}"
41,2,2,talk,Ryuji,,"@Shizue, yes. The glasshouse crew is full strength, but the modelling side is hollowed out.","{""words"":15,""mentions"":[""Shizue""],""raw_differs"":false}"
42,2,2,talk,Victoria,,"@Benjamin, the free simulation hours help, but if the spectroscopy is down, the data is useless.","{""words"":16,""mentions"":[""Benjamin""],""raw_differs"":false}"
43,2,2,talk,Benjamin,,"@Victoria, the glasshouse subsidy is a relief, but the animal costs are a major hurdle for B.","{""words"":17,""mentions"":[""Victoria""],""raw_differs"":false}"
44,2,2,talk,Midori,,"@Victoria, the spectroscopy issue is serious. But the field site loss suggests D is high-risk unless we use indoor samples.","{""words"":20,""mentions"":[""Victoria""],""raw_differs"":false}"
45,2,3,talk,Shizue,,The portfolio review confirms it. Live breeding colonies overrun schedules twice as often. B is risky.,"{""words"":16,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
46,2,3,talk,Ryuji,,"With modelling analysts gone, C is in trouble. We need simulation, but we lack the people to run it.","{""words"":19,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
47,2,3,talk,Victoria,,"The glasshouse is booked solid. A is feasible, but we must be careful with the timeline.","{""words"":16,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
48,2,3,talk,Benjamin,,"A uses the glasshouse, which is subsidized. C uses simulation, which is free, but we lack the staff.","{""words"":18,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
49,2,3,talk,Midori,,"D relies on field sites that are failing. Unless we pivot to indoor samples, D is too exposed to external shocks.","{""words"":21,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
50,2,4,talk,Shizue,,I am leaning towards A. It uses the stable glasshouse crew and avoids the breeding colony risks.,"{""words"":17,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
51,2,4,talk,Ryuji,,I agree. A is the most robust given our staffing gaps. C is too dependent on the missing analysts.,"{""words"":19,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
52,2,4,talk,Victoria,,"A is the safest bet. The glasshouse is busy, but it is operational. The spectroscopy issue affects C more.","{""words"":19,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
53,2,4,talk,Benjamin,,A is financially sound. The glasshouse subsidy covers the main cost. C is a gamble on staffing.,"{""words"":17,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
54,2,4,talk,Midori,,I support A. It avoids the field site risks of D and the staffing risks of C. It is the balanced choice.,"{""words"":22,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
